# The same refusal through the subcommand the gate calls. Reading the queue is
# what refuses, so every way in refuses alike; what this pins is that the gate's
# way in is one of them, and that a refusal reaches its exit code rather than
# being swallowed by the summary line.
run check
exit 3
file tasks/M1-01.md
	---
	id: M1-01
	owns:
	  - crates/normalize
	depends_on: []
	exclusive: no
	---
file tasks/M1-02.md
	---
	id: M1-02
	owns:
	  - crates/normalize
	depends_on: []
	exclusive: no
	---
stderr
	tasks.sh: tasks/M1-02.md: owns crates/normalize, which overlaps the crates/normalize that M1-01 owns, and neither depends on the other
